{"round": 1, "slug": "c1-pd-log-strip", "verdict": "precise-obstruction", "claim": "Restatement, score 0. For 1/2 < sigma < 1 the Fourier transform of u -> log zeta(sigma+iu) is: prime atoms minus the density (e^{(1-sigma)x}-1)/x plus one term of the same shape per zero right of sigma (contour-shift sketch; confirmed numerically to 1e-7 for zeta at heights 0, 1e3, 1e5 and for E_a with 14 zero terms). Raw positive-definiteness fails for every sigma < 1 (about half of Gram eigenvalues negative). The compensated property CP(sigma) is equivalent to \"no zeros with Re s > sigma\"; the proof of CP => zero-free uses only the half-line with no primes. No sigma-transport: the PD kernel ends at the pole on the diagonal. Proofs are unrefereed outlines. No zero statement claimed.", "aboutZeros": false, "refHolds": true, "threeReferees": false, "allHold": true, "r1": false, "r2": false, "r3": false, "r4": false, "score": 0, "errors": "Report: tmp/research-2026-11-16/push/c1-pd-log-strip/REFEREE.md. No error reverses the verdict (restatement, score 0). Theorems 1-3 were re-derived by hand (Frullani/Laplace forms, pole term via an independent split, kappa and e^x closed forms, the cutoff/dominated-convergence limit) and the Theorem 1 identity was rerun with independent code on new cases (zeta at sigma 0.7 and 0.55, widths 0.6/0.8, heights 0.3, 30, 2000; E_a at sigma 0.65 with zero", "surviving": "For 1/2 < sigma < 1 the Fourier transform of the horizontal-branch u -> log zeta(sigma+iu) is the prime atoms A_sigma minus the density (e^{(1-sigma)x}-1)/x plus one term m e^{i gamma x}(e^{(beta-sigma)x}-1)/x per zero right of sigma (explicit formula in integrated form; contour-shift sketch, re-derived and confirmed numerically to about 5e-7 on new cases including E_a with zero terms). Raw positive-definiteness fails on every such line by the Omega-theorem argument, although small Gram matrices near sigma = 1 can be positive-definite. Positivity of the compensated transform CP(sigma) is equivalent to no zeros with Re s > sigma, and the direction CP => zero-free uses only the half-line x < 0 with no primes. Three named sigma-transport mechanisms fail at the pole; a general no-transport theorem is not proved. The lane is a restatement with score 0; no statement about zeros of zeta is made; R1-R4 are all false.", "lesson": "Left of the pole, positivity of the compensated transform splits into a support (causality) condition on the prime-free half-line, which is the zero-free statement itself, and a sign condition on the atoms, which is automatic. Sign-positive twists, Beurling and E_a (sigma > 1/2+a, measured) pass, so the property separates zeta from them only through their zeros. Fixed-order minors hold under zero-freeness but do not exclude a single zero (reasoning only).", "followUp": "None on this route. Bookkeeping: close OBSTRUCTION_MAP section 6 item 8 as a restatement and extend row P29. If a referee pass is wanted, check Theorem 1 (contour shift details) and Theorem 3 (cutoff limit); six controls were assessed structurally, not run. Server not used.", "file": "tmp/research-2026-11-16/push/c1-pd-log-strip/RESULT.md", "kind": "explore", "refereeHolds": true, "referees": 1, "restatement": true, "knownArchitecture": false, "brokenProved": 0, "lemmasDecided": 0, "obstructionKind": "restatement-of-rh"}
{"round": 1, "slug": "c1-shear-hecke-fricke", "verdict": "precise-obstruction", "claim": "Lane killed by its decisive test. Proved: [V_n, G_tau] = G_tau(exp(tau l(u+l)/4) - 1)V_n with l = log n; the leading term is ad(u^2/16), with trivial kernel for each single p, so nothing is characterised jointly. The eigen-defect has tau-order exactly 1 iff the coefficients are completely multiplicative at p, else 0; no higher order exists. Measured for p <= 47: zeta has order 1.00, and so do completely multiplicative sign twists, zeta(s+0.3), a unimodular twist and Beurling; T-NR fails at one prime only. The statement is an equality (multiplicativity restated), not an inequality. w_4 on the X_0(4)(R) arc is u -> -u and gives evenness only. Pivot: the sheared Hecke operator is a multiplier composed with a real translation; the resulting prime expansion converges on the critical line only for tau < 0, where non-real zeros exist (Rodgers-Tao).", "aboutZeros": false, "refHolds": true, "threeReferees": false, "allHold": true, "r1": false, "r2": false, "r3": false, "r4": false, "score": 0, "errors": "Negative verdict (precise-obstruction, all flags false) holds; four corrections. (1) Lemma 2 is labelled \"PROVED for whole-line L^2 norms\", but for the controls without a functional equation (zeta(s+0.3), unimodular twist, Beurling, Liouville) the kernel is not in L^2(R) (grows like e^(-0.1u) or e^(-u/4) at -inf, or decay unknown), so the whole-line defect is undefined. The lemma is correct for windowed norms because the residual identity is pointwise; then sigma is window- and p-dependent (2.91", "surviving": "For windowed L^2 norms (whole-line only for kappa-type kernels): [V_n, G_tau] = G_tau(exp(tau l(u+l)/4) - 1)V_n; the eigen-defect of 1 - lambda V_p under the Gaussian shear has tau-order exactly 1 with coefficient (log p/4)*sigma when c(pm) = lambda c(m) for all m and K_p != 0, and order 0 otherwise. Zeta, zeta(s+a), completely multiplicative sign twists and unimodular twists all have order 1 (confirmed independently to 4-5 digits), so the test does not separate zeta and the statement is an identity (multiplicativity at p), not an inequality. w_4 on the X_0(4)(R) arc is u -> -u and gives evenness only. For tau < 0 the termwise prime expansion of H_tau converges absolutely at every z and holds equally for a sign-twisted control; that it cannot be carried to tau = 0 is a heuristic supported by Rodgers-Tao, not a theorem. No requirement R1-R4 is established; nothing about zeros of zeta is claimed.", "lesson": "The Hecke eigen-property is carried along by the shear (conjugated operators keep it at every tau); the only thing special at tau = 0 is that Hecke operators are translations, which is \"completely multiplicative ordinary Dirichlet series\" restated. Defect order in tau is 0 or 1 and cannot separate zeta from any completely multiplicative control. tau = 0 is the boundary of the sheared prime expansion's convergence domain, approached from the side where the conclusion is false, with l^1 size about exp(1/(4|tau|)).", "followUp": "None on this lane. Close OBSTRUCTION_MAP section 6 item 11 under the Gaussian-shear reading. The one untested reading is theta off the arc (Re tau_mod != 0) with the full T(p^2); the referee equivalence T(p^2)-eigen iff c(pm) = c(m) suggests the same outcome. No server job was run.", "file": "tmp/research-2026-11-16/push/c1-shear-hecke-fricke/RESULT.md", "kind": "explore", "refereeHolds": true, "referees": 1, "restatement": true, "knownArchitecture": false, "brokenProved": 0, "lemmasDecided": 0, "obstructionKind": "multiplicativity-identity-not-inequality"}
{"round": 1, "slug": "c1-multidelay-bohr-section", "verdict": "precise-obstruction", "claim": "The lead is closed in the forms tested. PROVED (author, unrefereed): (1) a block of prime sections has rank-one additive J-defect, so joint contractivity is the scalar inequality Re U_P <= Re G and every twist is a torus translation; (2) Theorems A/B: for finite P no positive-real function of several prime delays can be split off xi'/xi (weighted first moment of zeta over its poles has real part >= (T/2pi) Re H(0)); (3) no multi-delay inner multiplicative factor of S; (4) the sharp joint section fails at fixed sigma<1 for all large Q (pole term). MEASURED: at sigma=1/2 true phases fail at the same prime as every twist (Q11 count); in sigma zeta does better (0.76 vs 0.90-0.98, t in [1e3,1e4], Q=300). The Fejer-tapered section is contractive for zeta and fails for twists, but that is an explicit-formula identity equivalent to RH, without multiplicativity. General J-inner linear-fractional sections, infinite P and the DH analogue stay open.", "aboutZeros": false, "refHolds": true, "threeReferees": false, "allHold": true, "r1": false, "r2": false, "r3": false, "r4": false, "score": 0, "errors": "No PROVED step failed re-derivation; six corrections, none changes the verdict. (1) Lemma 1 is called a \"classification\" and item 1 says a block has \"no matrix or torus freedom\": true only inside the unipotent family M(Z) fixed by the chain convention; general J-inner sections are open. (2) \"Every sign and unimodular twist violates the tapered section for Q>=100 at sigma=0.5 and 0.6\" is window-specific: my rerun (Q=200, t in [2000,6000], new seeds) reproduces the separation at sigma=0.5 (zeta -0", "surviving": "Precise obstruction, confirmed. For finite P and H holomorphic with Re H >= 0 on the polydisc, the weighted first moment of zeta over the poles of a lossless h = H((p^{-z})) is (T/2pi)(H(0) + conj h(1/2)) + o(T), with real part at least (T/2pi) Re H(0). So no such h other than an imaginary constant can be split off xi'/xi as a positive-real summand (Theorem B; its hypothesis implies RH, its proof does not assume it). No bounded multi-delay factor divides S (trivial, from gamma growth). Within the unipotent chain family the joint defect is rank one and additive, so joint contractivity is the scalar inequality Re U_P <= Re G and twists are torus translations. The sharp joint section fails at fixed sigma < 1 for all large Q by the pole term (classical). The Fejer-tapered section is an explicit-formula identity equivalent to RH that uses no multiplicativity; its measured separation of zeta from twists holds at sigma = 1/2 on the windows tried and is window-dependent at sigma = 0.6. Infinite P, general J-inner sections and the DH analogue remain open. No progress on RH and no statement about zeros of zeta.", "lesson": "Sections of the prime chain commute and have rank-one additive defect, so grouping primes adds no freedom: joint contractivity is the largest-value inequality itself. What separates zeta's phases from twists is one-sidedness of the prime sum coming from the zero measure via the functional equation (visible with Fejer weights), not Kronecker structure of finitely many primes; that is a restatement of RH. Multiplicativity entered only in a no-go (point evaluation of H at p^-1/2).", "followUp": "Referee Theorem A step (iii) (off-diagonal and horizontal error terms; checked numerically to 1-3 percent at T=6000 only), then update row Q12 and item 7 of the obstruction map. The remaining open case is a J-inner linear-fractional section in several delays with Theta(0)=M(c0)U0, c0>0. No server run needed; none was used.", "file": "tmp/research-2026-11-16/push/c1-multidelay-bohr-section/RESULT.md", "kind": "explore", "refereeHolds": true, "referees": 1, "restatement": false, "knownArchitecture": false, "brokenProved": 0, "lemmasDecided": 0, "obstructionKind": "restatement-of-rh"}
{"round": 1, "slug": "c1-leeyang-chain-closure", "verdict": "partial-result", "claim": "Lemma decided: the closure of pair-ferromagnet laws is a proper subclass of the Lee-Yang class. Fixed degree: chain set = complete-graph set = Lee-Yang set for n = 2, 3, 4 (proved, explicit inverse, exact grids). For n = 5 it is proper: (w+1)(w^2-w/2+1)^2 and (w+1)^3(w^2-4w/3+1) are Lee-Yang but violate linear inequalities valid on every pair ferromagnet (FK cone: coefficients are multi-affine in e^(-2J)); exact separators also for n = 6..9. Limit sense: GHS holds on the closure and fails for (w^2-(44/23)w+1)(w+1)^44 (exact rational certificate). No zero information: GHS holds for Phi (recalled) and for DH (measured in parent lane). The three parent repairs are written with proofs; off-line zeros of Dirichlet stages N = 2, 3, 5, 10 are certified in ball arithmetic. T-NR run at stage level: fails uncoupled, passes heat-coupled at q = 29, c = 2.5. Not refereed.", "aboutZeros": false, "refHolds": true, "threeReferees": false, "allHold": true, "r1": false, "r2": false, "r3": false, "r4": false, "score": 0, "errors": "No PROVED step failed; all were re-derived by hand or rerun (report: tmp/research-2026-11-16/push/c1-leeyang-chain-closure/REFEREE.md). Corrections: (1) \"Phi in cl(L) is strictly stronger than RH\" is overstated: only the class inclusion is proved strict; for the single law Phi the converse failure is not shown (GHS, the one separating condition, holds for Phi). (2) R1=true is not supported: the decided lemma (Lemma 1, Theorem B, separators n=5..9, ", "surviving": "Partial result, no information on zeros of zeta. (1) For n = 2, 3, 4 the chain set, complete-graph set and Lee-Yang set coincide (proved, explicit inverse). (2) For n = 5..9 explicit Lee-Yang polynomials with nonnegative coefficients, e.g. (w+1)(w^2-w/2+1)^2 and (w+1)^3(w^2-4w/3+1), lie outside the Fortuin-Kasteleyn cone K_n and so are not laws of any zero-field pair ferromagnet of total weight n, hidden spins allowed (proved, exact). (3) GHS holds on the weak closure of pair-ferromagnet laws and fails for (w^2-(44/23)w+1)(w+1)^44 at w = 108/55 (exact rational), so the closure is a proper subclass of the Lee-Yang class. (4) Repaired Thm 6(d') holds for all t <= 0 for Dirichlet stages N = 2, 3, 4, 5, 10 (off-line zeros in the strip certified in ball arithmetic); Prop 7(b) restricted to the Lee-Yang class and the Curie-Weiss correction hold. (5) T-NR fails the uncoupled stage and passes the heat-coupled stage at q = 29, c = 2.5 (measured, confirmed to T = 120). Membership of Phi in the closure would imply RH; whether it is strictly stronger for Phi is not established. GHS does not separate zeta from DH (measured).", "lesson": "Ferromagnet membership is strictly stronger than Lee-Yang, but the conditions that separate them (GHS, FK cone) are kernel-side positivity that Phi and DH both satisfy, so they give no handle on zeros. R1 holds at stage level only (product over prime sites; DH stage 55 of 55 off the line; Beurling energies also pass). R3 fails: U, sign twists and Beurling pass every stage, and E_a and T-NR pass at small log M through heat contraction.", "followUp": "Referee this lane first: step 2.1(c) (Laguerre-Polya closure under e^(-lambda D^2) at Gaussian decay) and the GHS-with-hidden-spins input are recalled, not opened. The one remaining open item is whether P0 (atoms +-5 mass 1, +-1 mass 5/4) is a weak limit of ferromagnets with many small spins; it passes every GHS test tried and the FK cone does not apply to limits. Low priority: it carries no zero information.", "file": "tmp/research-2026-11-16/push/c1-leeyang-chain-closure/RESULT.md", "kind": "deepen", "refereeHolds": true, "referees": 1, "restatement": false, "knownArchitecture": false, "brokenProved": 0, "lemmasDecided": 1, "obstructionKind": "kernel-positivity-shared-with-controls"}
{"round": 1, "slug": "c1-missing-controls", "verdict": "partial-result", "claim": "Filter repair, no RH attack. A: sign twists (TW+, TW-) run in seven h-lanes: the DH-failing step (joint Hecke eigen-object; Dirichlet inverse = mu a) passes exactly in six, is not formulable in h-klein-fricke; the twist fails at the functional-equation step instead. No mechanism passes a control in full. B: backward heat zeros measured (lowest off-line: 415.256 at tau=-0.1, 111.694 at -0.5, 49.715 at -2; Re s>1 from height 60.7). In stack-feasibility heat passes (i),(iii),(vi), fails (ii) only as data mismatch, like DH. In sq-canonical-det the outcome follows from the lane's Lemma A; Ritz run of K[t] not done. C: Arb certificate: DH theta kernel positive, strictly log-concave, Csordas-convex on all u>=0. D: S(T) - (1/pi) arg zeta(1/2+a+iT) is unbounded both ways for fixed 0<a<=1/2, relative to Selberg's CLT and Bohr-Jessen (cited, not re-read); fixed shifted interlacing partners are closed. Unrefereed.", "aboutZeros": false, "refHolds": true, "threeReferees": false, "allHold": true, "r1": false, "r2": false, "r3": false, "r4": false, "score": 0, "errors": "E1 (wrong statement, FLAGS last paragraph and weakest step 3): the result says no sign twist with a false zero conclusion was constructed and that the only example in the push is the float64 U_R. Its own twist TW- b (Liouville) is an exact example for \"all zeros in the open strip lie on Re s = 1/2\": sum lambda(n) n^-s = zeta(2s)/zeta(s) has zeros at rho/2 (referee: |F| = 5.5e-31 at 1/4 + 7.06736257087i, |zeta| = 1.126 there, so not cancelled). These zeros lie in Re s < 1/2, so \"no exact twist wi", "surviving": "Partial result (filter repair, no RH attack) holds with corrections E1, E2. Confirmed: (A) six completely multiplicative sign twists pass the DH-failing coefficient steps exactly (identities); finite twists have zeta's zeros in the open strip (re-derived); failure at the functional-equation step is proved for finite twists, classical for degree-1 type, measured otherwise. (B) Backward heat off-line zeros confirmed by an independent mpmath trapezoid quadrature at 110/300 digits: tau=-0.1: 0.0215579+415.2562790i, 0.0609152+540.4697362i, 0.1402531+564.3693091i; tau=-0.5: 0.1981079+150.6922461i, 0.3432579+221.2268408i; tau=-2: 0.2485774+49.7154476i, 0.6247240+60.7078706i (Re s = 1.125); tau=-2 count on [1,62] is 10 sign changes + 2 pairs = 14 = zeta count; L_tau - L_0 mismatch values reproduced; sq-canonical-det outcome is relative to the lane's Lemma A plus measured zeros, Ritz run not done. (C) Arb certificate for the DH theta kernel (positive, strictly log-concave on u >= 0, u L'' - L' < 0 on u > 0) reproduced with a different split (U0 = 3/2, 47 terms, 256 bits); tail bound and the L'''' step re-derived; evenness is a classical input. (D) D1 (S(T) - (1/pi) arg zeta(1/2+a+iT) exceeds M and is below -M on proportions tending to 1/2 each, fixed 0 < a <= 1/2) and D2 (no fixed shifted pair is an interlacing partner of Xi) hold relative to Selberg's CLT and Bohr-Jessen; Stirling constant a*pi/4 checked numerically; height-dependent shifts and non-shift partners remain open. No statement about zeros of zeta; nothing assumes RH or restates verified height.", "lesson": "Every missing control gave the inferred outcome. The twist and DH fail complementary steps (functional equation vs Euler product), so step-level differences are expected and are not leads. A finite twist has zeta's zeros in the strip, so it controls only statements using zeta's archimedean side; a twist with a false zero conclusion needs infinitely many primes and none is refereed. Grid checks of the DH Csordas condition could not decide near u=0 (third-order vanishing); the certificate needed the fourth log-derivative plus evenness.", "followUp": "Referee Part D (re-read Selberg CLT and Bohr-Jessen statements, check the argument convention), then a Ritz run of K[t] for the heat family at tau=-2 (degree p > 100 t) to measure T*; optionally construct a TW+ or TW- twist with a zero in 7/8 < Re s < 1 to make the twist a true control for the 7/8 mechanism.", "file": "tmp/research-2026-11-16/push/c1-missing-controls/RESULT.md", "kind": "repair", "refereeHolds": true, "referees": 1, "restatement": false, "knownArchitecture": false, "brokenProved": 0, "lemmasDecided": 0, "obstructionKind": "filter-repair"}
{"round": 1, "slug": "c1-leeyang-krein-limit", "verdict": "precise-obstruction", "claim": "Semicontinuity of rank Gamma_- under bounded weak-* convergence is proved, but it does not give R4. In the stages' self-dual (conductor M) normalisation every stage symbol is inner and tends to the constant -1 on Re z>0, for zeta phases and every unimodular twist (proved; measured 2/(1+alpha L/2)). With the gamma factor and pole renormalisation the stage block has about one pole per stage zero (rank 27..118 for P=13..47, same for zeta, U, random U; measured), so liminf rank is infinite. In the strip topology (stage/zero-free normaliser, locally uniform) rank-0 semicontinuity is Hurwitz: RH holds iff pole-renormalised Euler stages are locally bounded on the strip (other direction via von Koch). U needs a normaliser with a pole at its zero. Proving boundedness for zeta is RH (PNT error term); the Lee-Yang inequality gives a bound weaker than the trivial one.", "aboutZeros": false, "refHolds": true, "threeReferees": false, "allHold": true, "r1": true, "r2": false, "r3": false, "r4": false, "score": 1, "errors": "All PROVED steps (Thm A, B, C/C1, D, section 5 comparison) were re-derived and confirmed; no RH assumption, no verified-height restatement, Theorem D explicitly not claimed as new. Four defects, none changing the verdict: (1) Section 5 asserts |D_M(1+delta+it)| <= zeta(1+delta) without justification (w(n) >= 1 up to n^(1/2), termwise bound diverges); numerically true (sup 3.8-4.0 vs zeta(1.1)=10.58 for P=23,41,53) and fixable by integrating to sigma'=3/2+delta; conclusion (exponent of order P, w", "surviving": "Precise obstruction holds. Rank Gamma_- is lower semicontinuous under bounded weak-* convergence (proved). In the self-dual conductor normalisation every Lee-Yang stage symbol is inner and tends to -1 on Re z > 0 for zeta phases and every unimodular twist (proved relative to the refereed stage theorem; measured), so the semicontinuity chain carries no information. With gamma factor and pole renormalisation the stage symbol has one right-half-plane pole per stage zero, identical for zeta, U and random twists (measured, argument-principle counts; not proved for all P). In the strip topology, zeta is zero-free in Re s > theta iff the pole-renormalised Euler stages are locally bounded on {Re s > theta, s != 1} (proved: Hurwitz + von Koch; no novelty). The Lee-Yang inequality gives a stage bound with exponent of order P, weaker than the trivial bound (proved up to an unjustified constant). No statement about zeros of zeta is established; R4 is not supplied.", "lesson": "The Lee-Yang stage's index 0 is the inequality Re D_M'/D_M > -(log M)/2, which becomes empty as M grows; the conductor factor that makes stages self-dual erases the limit (symbol -> -1). Any limit step from zero-free product stages is Hurwitz plus local boundedness of pole-renormalised stages, which is RH restated as the PNT error term. r1 holds only at stage level (zero-free stages need the Euler product); the U control fails at the open step, not at a proved one. Primes up to 47 cannot show the convergence mode: the pole factor is within 15 percent of 1 there.", "followUp": "Test whether the multivariate (polydisc, per-prime weights) Lee-Yang property yields a one-sided upper bound on log|E_P(s) exp(E_1((s-1)log P))| in the strip better than the trivial exp(P^(1-sigma)/log P); the single-variable consequence is weaker than trivial. Otherwise none: do not re-attempt Krein-index semicontinuity as a limit step.", "file": "tmp/research-2026-11-16/push/c1-leeyang-krein-limit/RESULT.md", "kind": "combine", "refereeHolds": true, "referees": 1, "restatement": false, "knownArchitecture": false, "brokenProved": 0, "lemmasDecided": 0, "obstructionKind": "restatement-of-rh"}
{"round": 1, "slug": "c1-spinor-hecke-primitive", "verdict": "precise-obstruction", "claim": "No lower bound obtained. For finitely many primes the joint primitive decomposition is Moebius inversion by M_S = prod(1-U_p), bounded and invertible, acting on the zero module by an invertible diagonal; Q_L o M_S is congruent to Q_L, so a bound on jointly primitive tests is P10 itself, for zeta and every control. The Gram matrix of dilations does not contain the function's coefficients: no control can fail at orthogonalisation; it replaces the divergent product by log P only in mean over height (sup is the full product, Kronecker). For all primes the Hecke sum lies in the radical of Q_L. Decisive test (float64, prime side, dims 12-400): zeta min eigenvalue 0 within 3.2e-10 on full and primitive spans; DH, Liouville twist, E_a, T-NR negative on both, growing with test length. Primes up to 100 run on zero side for zeta/E_a/T-NR only; DH and twist at 25 primes not run.", "aboutZeros": false, "refHolds": false, "threeReferees": false, "allHold": false, "r1": false, "r2": false, "r3": false, "r4": false, "score": 0, "errors": "Report written to tmp/research-2026-11-16/push/c1-spinor-hecke-primitive/REFEREE.md. holds=false under the strict default: one PROVED sub-step is false as stated and one citation does not cover the stated domain. The negative verdict itself (no bound, all flags false) survives in corrected form.\n\n1. ERROR, Lemma 3(a) and the congruence claim. The domain is stated as compactly supported tests. M_S preserves compact support but its inverse Z_S does n", "surviving": "No lower bound is obtained; precise obstruction in corrected form. For a finite set S of primes and smooth compactly supported one-sided tests, M_S = prod(1-U_p) maps the class into itself with range of infinite codimension (not onto). Every test is nevertheless a simultaneous L^2 and Q limit of jointly S-primitive tests (referee repair, classical zero-free region). So inf Q/norm^2 over jointly primitive tests equals the infimum over all tests of the class: the primitive restriction neither helps nor costs, for zeta and for controls with zeros in a comparable region. That this bound is \"P10 itself\" is not established, because P10 is proved on a different test class.\n\nThe Gram matrix of dilations does not contain the function's coefficients. It replaces the divergent product by about log P only in mean over height; the sup over all heights is the full product, attained at tau = 0. All-prime Hecke sums Zw lie in the radical of the zero sum (known, P12). Lemma 2 (single-lag collapse on p-primitive tests) and the y_z defect formulas of Lemma 5 are correct identities.\n\nMeasured (float64): the sign of the minimum eigenvalue is unchanged by the primitive restriction for zeta, DH, Liouville twist, random signs, E_a and T-NR. DH is positive to rounding on a band without off-line zeros. Flags: R1 = R2 = R3 = R4 = false.", "lesson": "Hecke/Moebius operators are translation multipliers with no zeros in the open strip, so restricting a translation-invariant form (Weil/Q_L) to primitive tests is a congruence and changes nothing; the orthogonalisation step is function-independent. Any multiplicative mechanism must act where the cutoff fails to commute with Hecke (window defect), and that formula is also kernel-independent. Reusable tool: plib.py computes Q_L from primes alone in an orthonormal basis (Loewner form) for zeta and four controls, validated against zero sums to 1e-12.", "followUp": "None on this route. The only surviving adjacent question is OPEN and of largest-value type: the size of sum over zeros of |zeta_S(1/2+i gamma)|^2 |W(i gamma)|^2 against the norm of Z_S w as S grows (partial Euler products sampled at zeros). The result needs a referee pass on Lemmas 3-5 before the map row is closed.", "file": "tmp/research-2026-11-16/push/c1-spinor-hecke-primitive/RESULT.md", "kind": "combine", "refereeHolds": false, "referees": 1, "restatement": false, "knownArchitecture": false, "brokenProved": 1, "lemmasDecided": 0, "obstructionKind": "function-independent-congruence"}
{"round": 1, "slug": "c1-window-sdp-scaling", "verdict": "precise-obstruction", "claim": "Lead 23 closes: the joint window SDP equals the explicit-formula LP, so delta*_joint = delta*_EF for T in 1e2..1e6 and K in {6,12,24}. PROVED: the real-node Pick matrix is linear in the zero measure, on-line pairs add PSD terms, a quartet is 2P_t + R with R of order sigma^2, and the LMI never contains the primes. GRID-CERTIFIED (Arb): the remainders are dominated, eps*(N_win+7)/2 = 0.32 at (100,24) and below 4e-6 for T >= 1000, with eps ~ sigma^2 T^-4. CERTIFIED: 445 of 445 LP-feasible configurations pass Pick; 40 of 40 LP duals pass on the grid. Thresholds (MEASURED): no exclusion for L <= L*/2 at any height; exclusion starts between L* and 2L*; at 2L* the close-pair threshold (0.27 to 0.35, or all) follows gap*L/pi, not 1/log T. DH is accepted at its true quartet; sign-twisted primes give the same kind of exclusion sets.", "aboutZeros": false, "refHolds": true, "threeReferees": false, "allHold": true, "r1": false, "r2": false, "r3": false, "r4": false, "score": 0, "errors": "Referee report: tmp/research-2026-11-16/push/c1-window-sdp-scaling/REFEREE.md. PROVED steps (Lemma 1, Lemma 2, Proposition D as a conditional, complex-node remark) re-derived and confirmed; no RH assumption; counts 40/40 and 445/445 confirmed after de-duplicating the logs (47 and 469 raw lines).\n\nE1 (substantive, in the GRID-CERTIFIED/search part): the hypothesis eps*(N_win+7)/2 < 1 of Proposition D is FALSE at (T,K)=(100,24) for node sets outside ", "surviving": "Lead 23 closes as an obstruction: in the window model with outside zeros fixed to zeta's, the real-node Pick LMI of order K <= 24 adds nothing to the explicit-formula LP. PROVED: Pick matrix linear in the zero measure, on-line pairs PSD, quartet = 2P_t + R, LMI is a function of the zero configuration only. GRID-CERTIFIED for the five tabulated node families at all 15 (T,K) cells (product 0.32 at (100,24), below 4e-6 for T >= 1000); CERTIFIED 445/445 LP-feasible points pass Pick and 40/40 LP duals pass on the grid. Corrections: for general node sets the Proposition D hypothesis fails at (100,24) (product up to 6.4) and the T >= 1000 bound is 1.1e-5 not 4e-6; at (100,24) the equality rests on the direct single-quartet criterion (critical multiplicity >= 61 vs cap 19.5 on 22 node sets, grid values), node-uniform statement OPEN. Thresholds are MEASURED and model-dependent. DH accepted at its true quartet; twisted prime sides give the same kind of exclusion sets. No statement about zeros of zeta. R1-R4 all false.", "lesson": "A positivity LMI that is linear in the zero measure and monotone in on-line mass cannot couple to the explicit formula: it acts only where it already excludes the configuration with the window emptied (K of about 4 per zero below, i.e. the verified-height horizon), and it is blind to the prime data. Half-plane Pick kernels for Re z > a0 are implied by the support constraint. Weakest point: the domination constant is certified on a grid and on five node families plus 48 hill climbs, with only a factor 2.5 margin at (T,K) = (100,24).", "followUp": "none for lead 23. If the lane is revisited, the only untested variant is an LMI whose on-line terms are not sign-definite (the sector matrices P+-B of stack-feasibility section 6); they were not run here.", "file": "tmp/research-2026-11-16/push/c1-window-sdp-scaling/RESULT.md", "kind": "compute", "refereeHolds": true, "referees": 1, "restatement": false, "knownArchitecture": false, "brokenProved": 0, "lemmasDecided": 0, "obstructionKind": "linear-in-zero-measure-prime-blind"}
{"slug": "e1-burgers-cascade-anticausal", "round": 1, "kind": "explore", "verdict": "OBSTRUCTION: A functional that vanishes on the critical configuration and is monotone along the heat flow must decrease forward, so it only transports information from t < 0 (where the conclusion is false) to t = 0.", "refereeHolds": true, "referees": 1, "r1": false, "r2": false, "r3": false, "r4": false, "aboutZeros": false, "file": "push/e1-burgers-cascade-anticausal/RESULT.md", "nodes": ["resolvent-burgers-identity", "map-pos-16"], "tags": ["heat-flow", "burgers", "hankel", "flow-blindness"], "obstructionKind": "flow-blindness / wrong direction of monotonicity", "restatement": false, "knownArchitecture": false, "brokenProved": 0, "lemmasDecided": 0, "batch": "engine batch 1", "refereeFile": "push/e1-burgers-cascade-anticausal/REFEREE.md", "refereeNote": "All PROVED steps re-derived; (ii) is over zeros of D_t counted with multiplicity, not poles of S_t.", "controlsThatDefeat": ["backward heat: the functional is bounded only by values at t < 0 where the conclusion is false", "DH-type and E_a-type configurations: no proved step fails for them", "sign twists: prime-side flux zero for every twist (argued from Q4, not computed)"], "addedBy": "c2-repair-owed-passes"}
